Yes — there are **three** triggers that cause automatic pagination, even without user scrolling:

---

## Auto-pagination when there's nothing to show

The app **does not sit on an empty screen** — it automatically triggers pagination. Here's how:

### Trigger 1: Empty items after snapshot ([TimelineTableViewController.swift:60-62](ElementX/Sources/Screens/Timeline/TimelineTableViewController.swift#L60-L62))

```swift
var timelineItemsDictionary = ... {
    didSet {
        applySnapshot()

        if timelineItemsDictionary.isEmpty {
            paginatePublisher.send()   // ← fires pagination check
        }
    }
}
```

Every time the items dictionary is set (including the initial empty or near-empty state), if it's **empty**, `paginatePublisher.send()` fires. This triggers `paginateIfNeeded()` which sends `.paginateBackwards` to the view model.

### Trigger 2: First appearance ([TimelineTableViewController.swift:255-258](ElementX/Sources/Screens/Timeline/TimelineTableViewController.swift#L255-L258))

```swift
override func viewWillAppear(_ animated: Bool) {
    guard !hasAppearedOnce else { return }
    tableView.contentOffset.y = -1
    hasAppearedOnce = true
    paginatePublisher.send()   // ← fires on first display
}
```

The very first time the view appears, it unconditionally fires `paginatePublisher.send()`. Since the table is **flipped** (newest at bottom, content offset starts at top), an empty or short timeline will satisfy the offset check in `paginateIfNeeded()`.

### Trigger 3: Pagination state changes ([TimelineTableViewController.swift:111-115](ElementX/Sources/Screens/Timeline/TimelineTableViewController.swift#L111-L115))

```swift
var paginationState: TimelinePaginationState = .initial {
    didSet {
        paginatePublisher.send(())   // ← re-checks after each pagination completes
    }
}
```

After each pagination completes (`.paginating` → `.idle`), it re-checks whether more is needed. This creates a **self-sustaining loop**: paginate → get items → still not enough to fill screen → paginate again, until either the screen is full or `.endReached` is hit.

### The offset check that makes it work

`paginateIfNeeded()` at [line 423-435](ElementX/Sources/Screens/Timeline/TimelineTableViewController.swift#L423-L435):

```swift
if paginationState.backward == .idle,
   tableView.contentOffset.y > tableView.contentSize.height - tableView.visibleSize.height * 2.0 {
    coordinator.send(viewAction: .paginateBackwards)
}
```

The table is flipped, so `contentOffset.y` near `contentSize.height` means the user is near the **oldest** content. When there are few or no items, `contentSize` is tiny, so this condition is naturally satisfied — meaning pagination fires automatically.

### Summary

No cached events → empty `timelineItemsDictionary` → trigger 1 fires → `paginateIfNeeded()` sees small content size → `.paginateBackwards` → Rust SDK fetches from server → diffs arrive → items still don't fill screen → trigger 3 fires → repeat until screen is full or start of timeline reached.

